Subject: media: staging: ipu7: isys: write frames through the snooped path

ISYS output pins are configured with is_snoop = 0, so the IPU writes
captured frames without snooping the CPU caches. Userspace reads capture
buffers through cached mappings (libcamera's software ISP, CPU and GPU
upload paths alike), and on x86 dma_sync_*_for_cpu() does not invalidate,
so cache lines still holding a buffer's previous frame are read back as
stale data: 32-pixel, one-row runs of old pixels that show up as orange
and blue dashes along moving edges (Surface Pro 12 Intel, OV13858 and
IMX681, 1920x1080 through libcamera).

Set is_snoop for the output pin. The IPU7 MMU configurations already
include an IS_DATA_WR_SNOOP stream. With it the dashes are gone on both
cameras at unchanged frame rate.
---
--- a/drivers/staging/media/ipu7/ipu7-isys-video.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/ipu7/ipu7-isys-video.c
@@ -407,8 +407,12 @@ static int ipu7_isys_fw_pin_cfg(struct ipu7_isys_video *av,
 	output_pin->link.pbk_slot_id = IPU_MSG_LINK_PBK_SLOT_ID_DONT_CARE;
 	output_pin->link.dest = IPU_INSYS_OUTPUT_LINK_DEST_MEM;
 	output_pin->link.use_sw_managed = 1;
-	/* TODO: set the snoop bit for metadata capture */
-	output_pin->link.is_snoop = 0;
+	/*
+	 * Write frames through the snooped path: the CPU reads capture
+	 * buffers through cached mappings, and non-snooped writes leave
+	 * stale lines of the buffer's previous frame in the CPU cache.
+	 */
+	output_pin->link.is_snoop = 1;
 
 	/* output pin crop */
 	output_pin->crop.line_top = 0;
